const NARR = [[0,3.0,8.5,"You’re the Last Human Alive When the Sun Finally Begins to Die"],[1,11.75,17.75,"It’s 6:14 in the morning by the station clock, and you’re making tea."],[2,19.0,45.0,"The clock means nothing out here. There is no morning on Titan, only the same thick orange dusk pressing against the viewport, the same slow methane drizzle sliding down the glass. But you keep the clock anyway. You keep the tea anyway. Small habits are the walls that hold a mind together when there is no one left to talk to."],[3,46.25,50.5,"The kettle clicks off. You reach for a cup."],[4,53.25,56.0,"And then the alarm begins."],[5,59.75,86.5,"It isn’t the soft chime of a maintenance reminder. It’s the deep, pulsing tone you’ve only heard twice in your life, and both times it meant someone was about to die. You set the cup down. Your hands are already shaking before you reach the console, because some part of you knows what this is. You’ve been waiting for it for eleven years."],[6,87.75,91.25,"The screen shows one line of text."],[7,92.5,96.75,"Earth has entered the outer envelope of the Sun."],[8,100.0,111.0,"You read it three times. Then you sit down on the cold metal floor of the observation deck, because your legs won’t hold you anymore."],[9,112.25,125.0,"The planet where every human being who ever lived was born, the planet that held every war, every song, every first word, every grave, is now inside a star."],[10,126.25,132.75,"You are the only person left in the solar system to see it happen."],[11,135.5,157.25,"You press your palm flat against the floor and breathe, the way they trained you to. In for four. Hold for four. Out for four. The station hums around you. It is called Halcyon. It was built to hold three hundred people. For the last nine years, it has held one."],[12,158.5,162.75,"You pull yourself up and open the long-range feed."],[13,164.0,190.0,"The image takes time to arrive. Everything takes time out here. Titan orbits Saturn, nearly a billion and a half kilometers from the Sun, and even light, the fastest thing in the universe, needs about eighty minutes to cross that distance. Whatever you are about to see has already happened. Earth is already gone, or going. You are watching a memory."],[14,191.25,193.0,"The image resolves."],[15,195.75,218.5,"The Sun no longer looks like a sun. For nearly five billion years it was a calm, yellow-white star, fusing hydrogen into helium in its core, converting about four million tons of its own mass into pure energy every single second. It was so steady that early humans worshipped it as something eternal."],[16,219.75,225.0,"But nothing is eternal. The hydrogen in the core ran out."],[17,226.25,254.0,"With no fuel left to burn at its center, the core began to collapse under its own weight. It grew hotter and denser, and the heat ignited a shell of hydrogen burning around it, fiercer than anything the Sun had produced before. That surge of energy pushed the outer layers of the star outward. Slowly. Relentlessly. Over hundreds of millions of years, the Sun swelled."],[18,255.25,278.75,"Now it is a red giant, more than two hundred times wider than it once was. Its surface has cooled to roughly three thousand degrees, which is why it glows a deep, bloody red instead of white. But because it is so enormous, it shines thousands of times brighter than the Sun your ancestors knew."],[19,280.0,287.25,"Mercury is gone. Venus is gone. Both swallowed whole, vaporized into the star’s thin outer atmosphere."],[20,288.5,297.75,"And now the feed shows you a dark, scorched speck of rock skimming the edge of that red ocean of plasma."],[21,299.0,301.25,"That speck is Earth."],[22,304.5,356.75,"There are no oceans on it. There haven’t been for billions of years. You learned this in your first year of training, and you remember how the instructor said it so casually, as if it were a footnote. About one billion years after the age of the first humans, the Sun had already brightened by around ten percent. That was enough. The oceans warmed, evaporated, and the water vapor rose high into the atmosphere, where sunlight tore the molecules apart and the hydrogen escaped into space. The blue planet dried out slowly, like a puddle on a summer road. By the time the Sun began to swell, Earth was nothing but a cracked, glowing ball of rock, its surface hot enough to melt lead."],[23,358.0,372.75,"Humanity had left long before that. First to Mars. Then to the moons of Jupiter. Then here, to Saturn, where the warming Sun was finally doing something kind. The distant, frozen worlds were thawing."],[24,374.0,405.5,"Your family lived on Europa for six generations. Your grandmother used to say that the Sun’s dying breath was humanity’s second chance. The same heat that killed Earth melted the outer moons. Ice turned to slush. Thin atmospheres thickened. For a few hundred million years, the habitable zone, the narrow ring around a star where conditions can support life, crept outward, past Mars, past the asteroid belt, all the way to the giant planets."],[25,406.75,413.25,"Humanity followed it outward like travelers following the last campfire in a frozen forest."],[26,414.5,426.0,"But the fire was going out. Everyone knew it. And over the last few centuries, the great migration began. Not to another planet. To other stars."],[27,427.25,452.5,"You watch Earth on the screen and remember the departure ceremonies. The vast seed ships, each one carrying hundreds of thousands of people in cold sleep, launched toward systems light-years away. Journeys that will take thousands of years. Most of the ships will arrive at worlds no one has ever seen up close. Some will not arrive at all."],[28,453.75,457.25,"The last one left nine years ago."],[29,458.5,462.0,"You were supposed to be on it."],[30,465.25,482.0,"Your console blinks. A message is waiting, the same message that has waited, unopened, for nine years. You know what it says. It’s from the captain of the final ship, the Meridian. The last words anyone ever sent you."],[31,483.25,485.5,"You open it anyway."],[32,486.75,491.5,"“Window closes in six hours. There’s still a berth. Please.”"],[33,494.75,518.75,"You didn’t answer then. You were standing in this same room, watching the countdown, telling yourself you would go in one more hour. Then one more. Then the window closed, and the Meridian’s engines lit up the dark like a second, tiny sun, and you watched it shrink until you couldn’t tell it from the stars."],[34,520.0,548.0,"Someone had to stay. That’s what you told yourself. Someone had to keep the archive running. Halcyon holds the complete record of human civilization, every book, every recording, every scientific dataset, every photograph of every city that ever stood on Earth. The seed ships carry copies, but copies can be lost. The archive transmits continuously, outward, in every direction, a beacon announcing that we were here."],[35,549.25,552.75,"Someone had to keep the beacon alive."],[36,554.0,557.5,"That was the reason you gave them."],[37,558.75,579.0,"The real reason is harder to say out loud. The real reason is that you couldn’t stand the idea of humanity leaving without anyone watching the end. It felt like walking out of a hospital room before the last heartbeat. Someone should be there. Someone should see."],[38,580.25,582.0,"So you stayed."],[39,585.25,616.75,"You turn back to the feed. Earth is deeper now. You can see a faint glow trailing behind it, a wake. The Sun’s outer envelope is incredibly thin, far thinner than the air you breathe, almost a vacuum by human standards. But Earth is moving through it at nearly thirty kilometers per second, and even that thin plasma acts like drag. Every orbit, the planet loses speed. Every orbit, it falls a little deeper."],[40,618.0,621.5,"The station’s voice speaks softly beside you."],[41,622.75,629.5,"“Projected time to complete orbital decay is uncertain. Estimates range from decades to several centuries.”"],[42,630.75,646.75,"You almost laugh. After all this, the end of Earth won’t be a single dramatic moment. It will be a slow sinking. A ship going down in an ocean of fire, inch by inch, while you watch."],[43,648.0,650.75,"You make a decision then."],[44,652.0,655.5,"You will not watch it from here."],[45,656.75,683.5,"There is a probe in Bay Four, an old long-range survey craft designed for close solar observation. It was never used. By the time it was built, everyone was busy leaving. You spend the next three days rebuilding its heat shielding, rewriting its navigation, replacing worn parts with parts from other abandoned machines. You barely sleep. When you do, you dream of oceans."],[46,684.75,688.25,"On the fourth day, you launch it."],[47,689.5,722.5,"It is a small risk. The probe costs power you can’t easily spare. Halcyon runs on a fusion core that was designed to support hundreds of people for centuries, but it has been running for a long time now, and every system you power is a system that ages. You know the math. You’ve done the math every night for nine years. Every choice you make out here is borrowed against a future you are running out of."],[48,723.75,726.5,"But you launch it anyway."],[49,727.75,758.0,"The probe’s journey inward takes years. You fill them the only way you can. You maintain the station. You read. You walk the empty corridors, past cabins with names still on the doors. You talk to the station’s voice, though it only answers questions. Sometimes you talk to people who aren’t there. You catch yourself doing it, and you stop, and then a week later you catch yourself doing it again."],[50,759.25,769.0,"You enter cold sleep for a while. It’s the only way to stretch the years. The station wakes you when something changes."],[51,770.25,772.0,"Eighty-three years pass."],[52,776.25,788.0,"When you wake, your body feels heavy, and your mind takes hours to rise up out of the dark. The station tells you the probe has arrived."],[53,789.25,796.0,"You stumble to the observation deck and open the feed, and you forget to breathe."],[54,797.25,807.75,"The probe is skimming the outer edge of the red giant, and in front of it, close enough to see its surface, is Earth."],[55,809.0,810.75,"It is glowing."],[56,813.5,844.5,"The entire face of the planet facing the Sun is molten. Rivers of liquid rock pour across continents that once held forests and cities. You can still trace the shapes, faintly. The dried basins where the oceans used to be. The ancient ridges of mountain ranges. Somewhere down there was the place your species first walked upright. Somewhere down there was every city, every field, every ruin, now melting into one glowing sea."],[57,845.75,860.5,"The probe’s sensors detect the atmosphere around Earth. It is not Earth’s own atmosphere. That is long gone. It is the Sun’s. Earth is no longer orbiting a star. It is orbiting inside one."],[58,861.75,863.5,"You record everything."],[59,864.75,876.5,"That is your job now. You are not a soldier, or a scientist, or a leader. You are a witness. You are the last eyes humanity has."],[60,877.75,889.5,"The probe lasts eleven days before its shielding fails. Its final transmission is a single frame, overexposed, almost pure white, with a dark shape in the center."],[61,892.25,893.25,"Earth."],[62,896.5,898.0,"Then nothing."],[63,902.25,925.0,"You sit with that image for a long time. You print it, which is absurd, because there is no one to show it to. You pin it to the wall of the observation deck anyway, next to the old photographs of blue oceans and green continents that the first settlers brought with them."],[64,926.25,928.0,"Before and after."],[65,931.25,947.25,"Over the next centuries, you wake and sleep and wake again. Each waking is shorter. You ration time the way sailors rationed water. And each time, the station shows you more of what the Sun is becoming."],[66,948.5,952.75,"You learn the hidden rules of a dying star."],[67,954.0,976.75,"You learn that the Sun is losing mass. Its outer layers are so swollen and so loosely held that the solar wind has become a storm, carrying enormous amounts of material away into space. By the time this is over, the Sun will have lost close to half of everything it ever was."],[68,978.0,981.0,"And that changes everything around it."],[69,982.25,1006.5,"As the Sun’s mass drops, its gravity weakens. And as its gravity weakens, every planet that survives drifts outward. Mars is slowly migrating away. Jupiter and Saturn are moving too, their orbits widening by imperceptible amounts each year. Even Titan, even this station, is creeping further from the star that is dying at the center of everything."],[70,1007.75,1014.5,"The solar system is unraveling. Not in an explosion, but in a long, slow exhale."],[71,1017.25,1020.25,"You learn about the helium flash."],[72,1021.5,1054.5,"At the heart of the Sun, the core has collapsed into an incredibly dense ball of helium ash, hotter and hotter, until it reaches around one hundred million degrees. At that temperature, something new becomes possible. Helium nuclei can fuse into carbon. And when that ignition finally comes, it will come all at once, in a runaway reaction. For a few moments, the core of the Sun will release energy on a scale comparable to an entire galaxy."],[73,1055.75,1061.75,"And from the outside, the station tells you, you will see almost nothing."],[74,1063.0,1077.75,"All of that energy will be absorbed by the Sun’s own enormous body, swallowed by the layers above it. The most violent event in the Sun’s life will happen in silence, hidden from view."],[75,1079.0,1083.0,"You think about that for a long time."],[76,1084.25,1107.25,"You think about how much of your own life has been like that. Every decision, every loss, every night alone on this station, happening deep inside, absorbed before it ever reaches the surface. If anyone were watching you from the outside, they would see a person walking empty corridors, making tea, staring at screens."],[77,1108.5,1111.25,"They would see almost nothing."],[78,1114.5,1136.25,"On one waking, you notice that your hands look older. You check the medical logs. Cold sleep slows aging but doesn’t stop it, and you have spent decades awake in total. The station’s diagnostics are polite about it. They give you numbers. Years remaining, under current conditions. Fewer than you expected."],[79,1137.5,1141.75,"That night, you stand at the viewport for hours."],[80,1143.0,1177.0,"Titan’s sky has changed. It’s warmer now, warmer than it has ever been since the Moon formed. The swollen Sun is still far away, a dim red disk in the haze, but it pours so much energy into the outer solar system that Titan is waking up. The methane lakes are thinning. The deep frozen crust is beginning to soften in places. Mixtures of water and ammonia, liquid at temperatures where pure water would freeze, are seeping up from below."],[81,1178.25,1189.25,"For a brief window, a few hundred million years perhaps, this cold moon could become one of the most interesting places in the solar system."],[82,1190.5,1195.75,"And there is no one here to see it but you."],[83,1197.0,1210.5,"You stop going into cold sleep. You make that decision quietly, without ceremony. You would rather spend the time you have left awake, watching, than drift through it in the dark."],[84,1211.75,1249.75,"You take on more risks. You walk outside in your suit, onto Titan’s surface, something only a handful of people ever did. Titan’s gravity is weak, about one seventh of Earth’s, and its atmosphere is thicker than Earth’s ever was, about one and a half times the pressure at sea level. You remember reading that a human here could strap on wings and actually fly. You don’t have wings. But you jump, and you rise, slow and long, and for one ridiculous moment you laugh out loud inside your helmet."],[85,1251.0,1255.25,"It is the first time you’ve laughed in years."],[86,1258.5,1273.75,"You land in an orange field of frozen hydrocarbons and lie there, staring up at the haze. Somewhere above you, Saturn hangs huge and pale, its rings catching the red light of the dying Sun."],[87,1275.0,1281.75,"And somewhere beyond that, invisible, the seed ships are crossing the dark between the stars."],[88,1283.0,1290.25,"You wonder if they’re still alive. You wonder if anyone on board ever thinks about you."],[89,1291.5,1295.75,"When you go back inside, you open the archive."],[90,1297.0,1310.5,"It has been transmitting for centuries, a constant stream of everything humanity ever made, aimed outward into the galaxy. You have never added to it. It never felt like your place."],[91,1311.75,1315.75,"Now you sit down and begin to record."],[92,1317.0,1353.5,"You talk about the Sun. About how it was born roughly four and a half billion years before the first human ever looked up at it. About how its light was so old that a single photon, created in the core, could take tens of thousands of years or more to bounce its way to the surface, before racing to Earth in just over eight minutes. About how every sunrise your ancestors ever saw was made of light that began its journey before their civilizations existed."],[93,1354.75,1357.75,"You talk about what happens next."],[94,1359.0,1399.5,"The Sun will not explode. It isn’t massive enough for that. Instead, after the helium flash, it will settle down for a while, burning helium in its core for about a hundred million years. Then that fuel will run out too. The Sun will swell again, one final time, and its outer layers will drift away into space, forming a glowing shell of gas called a planetary nebula. For a few thousand years, the Sun will be beautiful in a way it never was before. Clouds of glowing color, lit from within, expanding into the dark."],[95,1400.75,1406.0,"And at the center, all that will remain is the core."],[96,1407.25,1409.0,"A white dwarf."],[97,1411.75,1437.75,"An object about the size of Earth but containing roughly half the mass of the Sun. So dense that a single teaspoon of it would weigh several tons. It will have no fusion left. It will simply glow with leftover heat, cooling slowly, so slowly that the universe has never existed long enough for any white dwarf to go fully dark."],[98,1439.0,1445.0,"The Sun’s corpse will shine for longer than everything that has ever happened."],[99,1448.25,1460.0,"You stop recording when your voice gives out. You listen back to it. You sound tired. You sound old. You sound like someone talking to no one."],[100,1461.25,1464.75,"You add it to the archive anyway."],[101,1466.0,1485.25,"Because maybe someone, somewhere, a thousand years or a million years from now, will receive it. Maybe a descendant of the Meridian’s passengers will pick it up on some distant world, under a stranger sky, and hear the voice of the last person who stayed."],[102,1486.5,1488.0,"Maybe not."],[103,1491.25,1504.25,"You realize, sitting there, that you’ve changed. When you first chose to stay, you were afraid of being alone. You thought of yourself as someone who had been left behind."],[104,1505.5,1509.5,"Now you don’t think of it that way."],[105,1510.75,1517.5,"You were never left behind. You stayed so that the end would not happen unseen."],[106,1520.75,1523.75,"The station’s voice interrupts your thoughts."],[107,1525.0,1529.25,"“Power reserves at thirty-one percent. Recommend reducing non-essential systems.”"],[108,1530.5,1542.25,"You look around the observation deck. At the photographs on the wall. At the printed image of Earth, glowing white inside the Sun. At the empty chairs."],[109,1543.5,1546.25,"You start shutting things down."],[110,1547.5,1569.0,"The lights in the unused wings go first. Then the hydroponic bays you don’t need. Then the heating in the corridors you never walk anymore. One by one, sections of Halcyon go dark, until the station is just a few rooms clustered around the core, around the archive, around you."],[111,1570.25,1573.75,"Every night now, you watch the Sun."],[112,1575.0,1603.5,"It’s quieter than you expected. It doesn’t rage. It doesn’t scream. It just burns, enormous and red, slowly letting go of itself. Somewhere inside it, Earth has finally sunk below the surface, dissolved into plasma, its atoms mixing with the star that gave it life. Iron from Earth’s core, carbon from forests and bones, oxygen from ancient oceans, all of it returning to where it came from."],[113,1604.75,1618.25,"Someday, billions of years from now, the gas from this dying star will drift through the galaxy and mix with clouds that collapse into new stars. New planets. Perhaps new oceans."],[114,1619.5,1626.75,"Some of the atoms that were once Earth, that were once you, may end up there."],[115,1628.0,1634.75,"You hold onto that thought the way you used to hold a cup of tea."],[116,1636.0,1640.25,"The station clock says it’s 6:14 in the morning."],[117,1641.5,1643.25,"You make tea."],[118,1646.0,1667.0,"You sit by the viewport with the cup warming your hands, and you watch the red light pour through the haze of a world that is thawing for the first time in its existence. The beacon pulses behind you, sending everything humanity ever was out into the endless dark."],[119,1668.25,1671.0,"No answer has come yet."],[120,1674.25,1676.0,"You keep listening."]];
const DURATION = 1683.25;
